that cannot be called human.

15:17.040 --> 15:19.670
Poems that revive the dead 
when read: the Banka.

15:18.610 --> 15:27.320
<b>\h\hBanka\h\h</b>

15:20.250 --> 15:22.830
Poems that manipulate 
people's hearts: the Soumon.

15:21.610 --> 15:27.320
<b>\hSoumon\h</b>

15:23.390 --> 15:25.730
Poems that can bring back 
a person's youth: the Zouka.

15:24.860 --> 15:27.320
<b>\hZouka\h</b>

15:25.730 --> 15:27.320
That's just to name a few.

15:27.810 --> 15:31.080
Number thirteen, Zouka. Author unknown.

15:31.570 --> 15:36.830
In the Man'yōshū, this poem is about a nobleman 
wishing for a lady's youth to return.

15:36.830 --> 15:41.040
"In the heavens, the sun and moon shine. 
You are like them in my thoughts,

15:36.830 --> 15:43.830
<b>m 0 0 l 100 0 100 100 0 100</b>

15:37.030 --> 15:43.830
<b>In the heavens</b>

15:38.290 --> 15:43.830
<b>The sun and moon shine</b>

15:39.410 --> 15:43.830
<b>You are like them in my thoughts</b>

15:41.040 --> 15:43.840
and I regret that with each 
new day, you grow older."

15:41.210 --> 15:43.830
<b>And I regret that with each new day</b>

15:42.540 --> 15:43.830
<b>You grow older</b>

15:43.840 --> 15:47.130
A poem that causes mysterious 
phenomena when read?

15:47.130 --> 15:49.090
I've never heard of any such thing!

15:49.090 --> 15:52.180
It's the moon god Tsukuyomi's 
Water of Youth.

15:49.550 --> 15:57.850
<b>m 0 0 l 100 0 100 100 0 100</b>

15:49.550 --> 15:57.850
<b>Tsukuyomi's Water of Youth</b>

15:52.800 --> 15:57.850
Still, this poem only begins to show its power 
